WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
When designing a layer for a conch net, you might have to pick, do you want a 1x3 filter

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.640
or 3x3 or 5x5 or do you want a pooling layer?

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.500
What the Inception network does is it says, why don't you do them all?

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.380
And this makes the network architecture more complicated but it also works remarkably well.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.140
Let's see how this works.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:27.880
Let's say for the sake of example that you have inputs a 28x28x192 dimensional volume.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:34.360
So what the Inception network or what an Inception layer says is instead of choosing what filter

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:40.520
size you want in a conv layer or even do you want a convolutional layer or pooling layer,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.000
let's do them all.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:50.960
So what if you can use a 1x1 convolution and that will output a 28x28xsomething.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:59.000
Let's say 28x28x64 output and you just have a volume there.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:07.600
But maybe you also want to try a 3x3 and that might output a 28x28x128 and then what you

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:15.760
do is just stack up this second volume next to the first volume and to make the dimensions

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.720
match up, let's make this a same convolution.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:26.520
So the output dimension is still 28x28 same as the input dimension in terms of height

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.920
and width, but 28x28 by in this example 128.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.920
And maybe you might say, well I want to hedge my bets, maybe a 5x5 filter works better so

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:47.640
let's do that too and have that output a 28x28x32 and again you use the same convolution

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:53.080
to keep the dimensions the same and maybe you don't want a convolutional layer, let's

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:59.000
apply pooling and that has some other output and let's stack that up as well.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:06.000
And here pooling output is 28x28x32.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.040
Now in order to make all the dimensions match you actually need to use padding for max

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.040
pooling.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.200
So this is an unusual form of pooling because if you want the input to have height and

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.600
width 28x28 and have the output match the dimensions of everything else also by 28x28

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:31.920
then you need to use same padding as well as a stride of 1 for pooling.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.680
So this detail might seem a bit funny to you now but let's keep going and we'll make

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.240
this all work later.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:46.320
But with an inception module like this you can input some volume and output in this

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.720
case you can see we add up all these numbers 32 plus 32 plus 128 plus 64 that's equal to

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.440
256.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:07.080
So you will have one inception module input 28x28x129 and output 28x28x256.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:13.800
And this is the heart of the inception network which is due to Tristan Zegady, Weylu Yantin

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.120
Zia, Pia Semine, Scott Reed, Dragomah Angeloff, Dimitri Erhan, Vincent Van Kook and Andrew

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.800
Rabinovich.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.360
And the basic idea is that instead of you needing to pick one of these filter sizes or

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.400
pooling you want and committing to that you can do them all and just concatenate all the

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.360
outputs and let the network learn whatever parameters it wants to use whatever combinations

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.680
of these filter sizes it wants.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.080
Now it turns out that there's a problem with the inception layer as we described it here

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.200
which is computational cost.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:54.440
On the next slide let's figure out what's the computational cost of this 5x5 filter resulting

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.280
in this block over here.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:07.320
So just focusing on the 5x5 part on the previous slide we had as input a 28x28x192 block and

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:15.400
you implement a 5x5 same convolution with 32 filters output 28x28x32.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.520
On the previous slide I had drawn this as a thin purple size but I'm just going to draw

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.720
this as a more normal looking blue block here.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:30.720
So let's look at the computational cost of outputting this 28x28x32.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:40.360
So you have 32 filters because the output has 32 channels and each filter is going to be

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:47.560
5x5x192 and so the output size is 28x28x32 and so you need to compute 28x28x32 numbers

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:54.560
and for each of them you need to do this many multiplications 5x5x192.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:06.600
So the total number of multipliers you need is the number of multipliers you need to compute

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:13.360
each of the output values times the number of output values you need to compute.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:19.640
And if you multiply out all these numbers this is equal to 120 million.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:25.920
And so while you can do 120 million multipliers on a modern computer this is still a pretty

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.720
expensive operation.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.040
On the next slide you see how using the idea of 1x1 convolutions which you learned about

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.240
in the previous video you'll be able to reduce the computational cost by about a factor of

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:45.240
10 to go from about 120 million multipliers to about 1 tenth of that.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.760
So please remember the number 120 so you can compare it with what you see on the next slide

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.800
120 million.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:06:01.800
Here's an alternative architecture for inputting 28x28x192 and outputting 28x28x32 which is

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.240
following.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:10.240
You're going to input the volume, use a 1x1 convolution to reduce the volume to a 16 channels

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:19.520
instead of 192 channels and then on this much smaller volume run your 5x5 convolution to

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.080
give you your final output.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.800
So notice the input and output dimensions are still the same.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:31.800
You input 28x28x182 and output 28x28x32 same as the previous slide.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.320
But what we've done is we've taken this huge volume we had on the left and we've shrunk

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:45.320
it to this much smaller intermediate volume which is only has 16 instead of 192 channels.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:55.580
Sometimes this is called a bottleneck layer.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.860
I guess because a bottleneck is usually the smallest part of something.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:06.860
So I guess if you have a glass bottle that looks like this then you know this is I guess

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.860
where the core goes.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:14.220
Then the bottleneck is the smallest part of this bottle.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.220
So in the same way the bottleneck layer is the smallest part of this network where you

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:23.380
shrink the representation before increasing the size again.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:27.380
Now let's look at the computational cost involved.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:32.860
To apply this 1x1 convolution we have 16 filters.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.380
Each of the filters is going to be of dimension 1x1x192.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:44.380
This 192 matches that 192 and so the cost of computing this 28x28x16 volume is going

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:51.580
to be well you need this many outputs and for each of them you need to do 190 pixels

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:54.380
and 192 multiplications.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:08:01.380
I could have written 1x1x192 and if you multiply this out this is 2.4 million.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:04.940
It's about 2.4 million.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:05.820
How about the second?

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:12.020
So that's the cost of this first convolutional layer.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:17.540
The cost of this second convolutional layer will be that well you have this many outputs

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:28.540
so 28x28x32 and then for each of the outputs you have to apply a 5x5x16 dimensional filter.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:36.540
So 5x5x16 and you multiply that out is equal to 10.0.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:41.260
And so the total number of multiplications you need to do is the sum of those which

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:45.260
is 12.4 million multiplications.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:49.420
And if you compare this with what we had on the previous slide you reduce the computational

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:58.420
cost from about 120 million multiplies down to about 1 tenth of that to 12.4 million multiplications.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:04.860
Oh and the number of additions you need to do is about very similar to the number of

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:11.060
multiplications you need to do so that's why I'm just counting the number of multiplications.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:15.340
So to summarize, if you're building a layer of a neural network and you don't want to

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:21.580
have to decide do you want a 1x1 or 3x3 or 5x5 or a pooling layer, the inception module

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:26.060
lets you say let's do them all and let's concatenate the results.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:30.700
And then we ran into the problem of computational cost and what you saw here was how using

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:36.620
a 1x1 convolution you can create this bottleneck layer thereby reducing the computational

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.580
cost significantly.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:43.380
Now you might be wondering does shrinking down the representation size so dramatically

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:47.020
doesn't hurt the performance of your neural network?

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:53.460
It turns out that so long as you implement this bottleneck layer within reason you can

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:59.340
shrink down the representation size significantly and it doesn't seem to hurt the performance

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.300
but saves you a lot of computation.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:08.260
So this is the key of the, these are the key ideas of the inception module.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:13.700
Let's put them together and in the next video show you what the full inception network

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:14.100
looks like.
